\section{Aetherius-9 Stress Testing Methods}
\label{sec:a9_methods}

The Aetherius-9 Engine was evaluated under a controlled multi-vector
stress testing protocol designed to probe numerical stability,
topological resilience, and distributed memory performance across the
Tetra-Chiral Conformal Manifold (TCCM) geometry solver. All tests were
executed in continuous-yield mode across a 1,024-node NUMA/MPI cluster.

\subsection{Stress Vector Definitions}
Four independent stress vectors were applied to the engine, each
targeting a distinct subsystem within the TCCM computational pipeline:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{ST-01: High-Intensity Neutron Flux Spike} —
    A neutron flux of $\Phi_n = 10^{28}\,\mathrm{n/m^2\,s}$ was injected
    into the Chiral Fluid Dynamics (CFD) module. The objective was to
    trigger high-frequency mesh refinement and evaluate AMR throttling
    behavior under extreme scalar twist density.

    \item \textbf{ST-02: Extreme Thermal Gradient Shock} —
    A thermal gradient of $\Delta T = 4.5 \times 10^{12}\,\mathrm{K/s}$
    was applied to the thermodynamic substrate coupling layer. The test
    monitored divergence triggers and timestep contraction within
    $\Delta t < 3$ solver iterations.

    \item \textbf{ST-03: Topological Manifold Pinch} —
    The metric determinant was driven toward collapse,
    $\det(g_{ij}) \rightarrow 10^{-18}$, to evaluate the TCCM metric
    tensor kernel and the Event-Horizon Guard responsible for clamping
    spatial boundaries and preventing singularity propagation.

    \item \textbf{ST-04: Maximum Memory Bandwidth Saturation} —
    Full NUMA node allocation (100\%) was enforced while sustaining
    MPI ghost-cell exchange at peak ingestion rates. The test validated
    buffer integrity, queue shedding, and synchronization latency.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Diagnostic Telemetry and Monitoring}
During each stress vector, the engine emitted real-time diagnostic
telemetry including AMR refinement levels, covariant divergence
residuals, metric determinant minima, ghost-cell synchronization
latencies, and entropy drift measurements. All telemetry was logged
through the Aetherius-9 continuous-yield monitor.

\subsection{Post-Stress Integrity Evaluation}
Energy-momentum conservation was verified across all distributed nodes,
with cumulative drift remaining below $10^{-12}$. Topological invariants
(Betti numbers $\beta_0$, $\beta_1$, $\beta_2$) remained within valid
TCCM bounds throughout the manifold pinch test, confirming geometric
resilience. No NaN propagation or boundary leakage was detected in any
downstream solver stage.